From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ThumbFree <thumbfree@localhost>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 23:12:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] parakeet: a session can run its graphs on the caller's
 persistent CPU pool

Every CPU graph compute without a threadpool starts and joins one of its
own, and the decoder builds a pool for every decode call: 4 worker threads
per encoder run and per decode with 5 threads, plus the mel's. A new
transcribe_session_set_threads(session, n_threads, threadpool) sets the
session's thread count and, optionally, a persistent ggml CPU pool made by
the caller (the CPU backend's ggml_threadpool_new, whose thread 0 is the
thread that calls transcribe_run), so the caller can keep its workers
alive, place them on chosen CPUs and give their ids to a performance hint
session. Parakeet runs the encoder on it (attached to the model's CPU
backends for the compute, then detached, which pauses it) and the
decoder's encoder projection; the decoder's step graphs too when they use
from 2 threads up to the session's count (one thread keeps its own pool,
which has no workers, and the session's pool is paused meanwhile); and the
mel on the session's thread count. Other models take only the count.
Without a pool nothing changes.
